.TH LINDA 1 2026-11-22 Linda "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linda \- a screen reader for the Linux desktop, based on NVDA
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linda
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B linda say
.RI [ options ] " text"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Linda Is Non-visual Desktop Access: NVDA, the screen reader most blind people use on Windows,
brought to Linux.
It reads programs through AT-SPI and speaks through PipeWire.
It takes its keys through
.BR linda-keyd ,
a system service that holds the keyboards in the kernel, wherever that service runs, and otherwise
from the X server in an X11 session.
Without the service, Linda in a Wayland session reads the screen but has no keys, and says so.
Started without options, it starts the screen reader, or, when a copy is already running for this
display, stops that copy and takes its place.
.PP
.B linda say
speaks one text through Linda's speech and audio and exits; see
.BR linda-say (1).
.PP
Linda is based on NVDA by NV Access and the NVDA contributors.
It is an independent project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by NV Access.
.SH OPTIONS
These are NVDA's options, as
.B linda \-\-help
lists them.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quit
Quit the copy of Linda that is already running.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-check\-running
Report whether Linda is running through the exit status: 0 if it is running, 1 if it is not.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-log\-file " \fILOGFILENAME\fR"
The file log messages are written to.
By default it is nvda.log in Linda's directory under the XDG state directory,
normally \fI~/.local/state/linda\fR.
Logging is always off in secure mode.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-level " \fILEVEL\fR"
The lowest level of message logged: 5 for secrets, 10 for debug, 12 for input and output,
15 for debug warnings, 20 for information and 100 for none.
The default is 20, or the level set in the configuration.
Logging is always off in secure mode.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config\-path " \fICONFIGPATH\fR"
The directory where all of Linda's settings are kept, normally \fI~/.config/linda\fR.
Secure mode always uses the default.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-lang " \fILANGUAGE\fR"
Use this language instead of the configured one, such as en for English.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-minimal
No sounds, no interface and no start message.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-secure
Start in secure mode.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-disable\-addons
Disable all add-ons.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-logging
Log at the debug level for this run alone, whatever other log level is given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-logging
Turn logging off for this run; a log level or debug logging given as well wins over it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sr\-flag
Do not change the system's screen reader flag.
.TP
.B \-\-register\-desktop
Make Linda the screen reader the MATE desktop starts at sign-in and from its accessibility
settings, say what was changed, and exit without starting the screen reader.
GNOME always starts Orca, so there is nothing to register there.
.TP
.B \-\-unregister\-desktop
Put MATE's choice of screen reader back to its defaults, Orca and off, say what was changed, and
exit.
.PP
The options \-\-install, \-\-install\-silent, \-\-create\-portable, \-\-create\-portable\-silent,
\-\-portable\-path, \-\-launcher, \-\-enable\-start\-on\-logon, \-\-copy\-portable\-config and
\-\-ease\-of\-access are NVDA's for Windows, where they install it, make portable copies of it and
start it at sign-in. Linda accepts them because NVDA does.
.PP
Two more are Linda's own, each given alone, and
.B linda \-\-help
does not list them.
.TP
.B \-\-session\-report
Print what decides how Linda runs in this session, and exit without starting the screen reader:
the variables the session's kind is decided from, the kind and the rule that decided it, the
desktop, and whether the socket of
.B linda-keyd
is there.
.TP
.B \-\-take\-keyboard
Ask the copy of Linda running in this Wayland session to take the keyboard back through
.BR linda-keyd ,
as after the emergency chord of Backspace, Escape and Enter held together, print how it went, and
exit: 0 if the running copy was asked, 1 if it could not be.
A copy in an X11 session cannot be asked this way; there Linda+Shift+F12 does it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/linda
Settings, profiles, speech dictionaries and add-ons.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/linda/nvda.log
The log of the last run.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
An installation knows where its libraries, eSpeak NG, ICU and openevv's driver are.
The variables LINDA_VBUF_LIB, LINDA_VBUF_ENGINE, LINDA_PWSINK_LIB, LINDA_ESPEAK_LIB,
LINDA_ESPEAK_DATA_DIR, LINDA_ICU_LIB_DIR, LINDA_ICU_VERSION_MAJOR, LINDA_OPENEVV_DRIVER_DIR and
LINDA_AUDIO_SINK, when set, name others instead; they are for development and testing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR linda-say (1)
.PP
https://github.com/Mudb0y/linda, where problems with Linda are reported; never report them to
NV Access.
